    "case_id": "677",
    "case_summary": "A 66-year-old female patient with osteoporosis presented with complaints of back pain. The pain was radiating to the left side and gradually worsening in recent months. The back pain had not improved despite left nephrectomy due to kidney stones about 1 year ago. The patient was taking alendronate 70 mg once a week for osteoporosis. Family history was unremarkable.\n\nPhysical examination revealed a scar of incision on the left lumbar region and mild left costovertebral angle tenderness. Neurologic examination showed negative babinski sign and normal deep tendon reflexes on both lower limbs. There were neither sensory loss nor motor neurologic deficit.\n\n- Laboratory tests: Comprehensive laboratory investigations including serum creatinine, calcium, phosphate, parathyroid hormone, thyrotropin and thyroid hormones were normal.- Imaging studies:  1. Lumbar spine X-ray: Demonstrated degenerative changes of the vertebral bodies  2. Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry: T-scores of both the lumbar spine and proximal femur were less than -3  3. MRI findings:     Image Title: T1-weighted sagittal image with contrast     Image Description: A 35 × 9 mm sized mass occupying the spinal canal at T8-T9 level. Well-circumscribed intradural extramedullary lesion, hypointense on T1-weighted images and heterogene hyperintense on T2-weighted images. The lesion compressing the spinal cord from posterior showed peripheral rim enhancement around the central cystic area after contrast media injection.- Histopathological examination:  Description: Tumor consisted of elongated spindle cells with vesicular nucleus arrayed in a palisade pattern among large cystic cavities lined by single-layer squamous epithelium. Markedly nuclear pleomorphism present in some tumor cells.  Immunohistochemical analysis: Neoplastic cells were negative for epithelial membrane antigen, pancytokeratin and desmin but diffuse positive for protein S-100. Ki67 proliferation index was 1%.",
